The {{nihongo|'''Three Unifiers of Mitoyagi'''|三英傑|San Eiketsu|lit. "Three Heroes"}} are three historical figures who brought about the unification of [[Mitoyagi]] (modern [[Okaiken]]) during the late [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] and early [[Seto-Tosa Period|Seto-Tosa period]]. The three unifiers—'''[[Ogasawara Terumasa]]''', '''[[Takigawa Kazumasu]]''', and '''[[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]]''' (later [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi]])—collectively ended over a century of civil war and established the foundation for the [[Honda Shogunate]], which ruled Okaiken for over 250 years.

The three men are remembered for their contrasting approaches to governance: Terumasa used revolutionary military tactics and brutal intimidation to break the power of the old order; Kazumasu imposed systematic administrative reforms through fear and strict social stratification; and Shigetada combined military genius with political legitimacy as the hidden Imperial Prince Fumihito to create a stable dual-government system.

==Background==
{{Main|Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Seto-Tosa Period}}

The Sengoku period (1467–1582) was characterized by the collapse of central authority following the [[Taiju War]] and the rise of regional warlords (''[[daimyō]]'') who competed for territorial control. The phenomenon of {{nihongo|''gekokujō''|下克上}}, or "the low conquering the high," saw traditional aristocratic families overthrown by their own vassals or peasant-born military commanders.

The period was further destabilized by:
* The [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] (1336), which had permanently divided the Sanese archipelago between the northern nation of Mitoyagi and the southern nation of [[Nakamizu]]
* The rise of religious militancy, particularly the [[Ikkō-ikki]] leagues
* The introduction of [[arquebus]] firearms from continental traders
* The weakening of the [[Minato Shogunate|Minato]] and [[Jofun bakufu]] governments

By the late 16th century, the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558—in which [[Ogasawara Terunobu]] abducted Prince Fumihito, son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]—set in motion the chain of events that would eventually lead to unification under the three warlords.

==Ogasawara Terumasa==
{{Main|Ogasawara Terumasa}}
[[File:Battle of Nagashino.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The [[Battle of Nagashino]] demonstrated Terumasa's revolutionary use of massed arquebus fire against traditional samurai cavalry.]]

'''Ogasawara Terumasa''' ({{circa|1540|1592}}), known as the {{nihongo|"Demon King of the Sixth Heaven"|第六天魔王|Dairokuten Maō}}, was the first of the three unifiers. Rising from a minor daimyo family in [[Mino Province]], Terumasa's career was defined by revolutionary military tactics and unprecedented brutality against the old order.

===Rise to power===
Terumasa's rise began with his decisive victory at the [[Battle of Mazama]] in 1581, where he annihilated the forces of [[Imazato Yoshimoto]] and reportedly displayed 3,000 severed enemy heads along the [[Tokaido]] road. In 1584, he committed the act that defined his legacy: the [[Burning of Mount Hiei|burning of the monastery complex at Mount Hiei]], which killed an estimated 20,000 people and demonstrated that spiritual authority would no longer be exempt from military power.

===Reforms and policies===
Terumasa implemented several revolutionary policies:
* '''Meritocratic promotion''': He elevated capable commanders regardless of birth, including the peasant-born [[Honda Shigetada]]
* '''{{nihongo|Rakuichi Rakuza|楽市楽座}}''': Free market policies that destroyed the monopolies of traditional merchant guilds
* '''Tenka Fubu''': The ideology of "Rule the Realm by Force"

===Death===
On June 21, 1592, while resting at [[Honto-ji Temple]] in [[Mito]] en route to suppress a rebellion, Terumasa was betrayed by his trusted general [[Takigawa Kazumasu]]. The [[Honto-ji Incident]] saw the temple burned and Terumasa forced to commit ''[[seppuku]]'' in the burning pagoda. His dream of unification died with him, but his administrative and military reforms laid the groundwork for his successors.

==Takigawa Kazumasu==
