'''Takigawa Kazumasu''' ({{circa|1525|1600}}), known for the {{nihongo|"Takigawa Terror"|滝川の恐怖|Takigawa no Kyōfu}}, was the second unifier. Originally a vassal of the [[Saitō clan]], he rose through the ranks to become one of Terumasa's most trusted generals before his infamous betrayal.

===Consolidation of power===
Following the Honto-ji Incident, Kazumasu moved rapidly to eliminate Terumasa's remaining loyalists. The [[Battle of Mirakawa]] and subsequent "Mirakawa Purge"—in which 500 retainers of [[Shibata Kazutomo]] were buried alive—established his reputation for ruthless efficiency. He governed through the {{nihongo|"Council of Four Elders"|四大老|Shi-tairō}}, comprising himself, [[Sawano Hiroyuki]], [[Yamagata Masakage]], and [[Maeda Toshiie]].

===Administrative reforms===
Kazumasu's most lasting contributions were administrative rather than military:
* '''{{nihongo|Taikō Kenchi|太閤検地}}''': A comprehensive land survey that centralized taxation
* '''{{nihongo|Katanagari|刀狩}}''': The "Sword Hunt" that disarmed the peasantry
* '''Social stratification''': Freezing the class system to prevent further ''gekokujō''

These reforms, while oppressive, created the bureaucratic foundation for the later Honda Shogunate.

===Death and succession crisis===
During the [[siege of Odawara|Siege of Katsuura]] against the [[Hōjō clan]] in 1600, Kazumasu was killed by a sniper's bullet. His death triggered the [[Battle of Tone]], the bloodiest single day in Sanese history, which would ultimately bring the third unifier to power.

==Honda Shigetada (Emperor Shirakawa)==
{{Main|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}
[[File:Battle of Tone.png|thumb|right|250px|The [[Battle of Tone]] (1600), the decisive engagement that established Honda Shigetada's supremacy.]]

'''Honda Shigetada''' (1556–1625), later known as '''Emperor Shirakawa''', was the third and final unifier. His true identity as '''Prince Fumihito'''—the Imperial Prince abducted during the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558—was revealed in 1601, giving him unparalleled legitimacy as both a military ruler and divine sovereign.

===Early life and discovery===
Following his abduction by the Ogasawara clan, the young prince was abandoned at [[Shōhei Temple]] and raised as a commoner named "Ichimatsu" by the monk [[Zen no Kishō]]. He was later adopted by [[Honda Shigetatsu]] of the rising Honda clan and trained as a samurai, earning renown as {{nihongo|"The Warrior who Surpassed Death"|死を超えた武者|Shi o Koeta Musha}}.

===Battle of Tone===
{{Main|Battle of Tone}}
Following Kazumasu's death, Shigetada led the Eastern Army against the Western Army of Takigawa loyalists. On October 21, 1600, over 160,000 men clashed at the [[Tone River]]. The battle was decided by the defection of [[Koyama Hidemoto]] from the Western Army, leading to the annihilation of the Takigawa coalition. Casualties exceeded 99,000, making it the bloodiest day in Sanese history.

===Imperial restoration and dual sovereignty===
In January 1601, [[Emperor Komei]] publicly recognized Shigetada as his son Prince Fumihito. In an unprecedented move, the Emperor declared Shigetada both Crown Prince and ''[[Sei-i Taishōgun]]'', merging civil and military authority. Upon Komei's death in 1610, Shigetada ascended to the Chrysanthemum Throne as '''Emperor Shirakawa'''.

The Emperor implemented the {{nihongo|''Edict of the Two Realms''|両界の令|Ryōkai-no-Rei}} in 1611, establishing:
* The Emperor as supreme sovereign from the Imperial Court
* The Shogun ([[Honda Shigetomo]], Shirakawa's son) as military administrator from [[Okami]]
* This {{nihongo|"Dual Sovereignty"|両主権|Ryō Shuken}} system balanced divine legitimacy with practical governance

===Death and legacy===
On June 6, 1625, the [[Kinzō Incident]] saw the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa by [[Ii Naotaka]]'s forces at [[Toyozawa Castle]]. The subsequent [[Battle of Ichijoudani]] on June 18, 1625, claimed the life of Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]]. However, the "Great Reconciliation" brokered by the dying Shogun's letter of forgiveness preserved the Honda Shogunate, which would rule until 1870.

