===Cultural impact===
The three unifiers remain central figures in Sanese historical memory:
* Their stories are frequently adapted in [[kabuki]], [[Noh theater]], and modern media
* The {{nihongo|"Sengoku Export"|戦国輸出}}—the emigration of disenfranchised samurai to [[Lurandia]]—was a direct consequence of the wars of unification
* The collective trauma of the period is credited with creating the modern Sanese aversion to civil disorder and preference for authoritarian stability

===The "fourth unifier" debate===
Some historians argue that [[Honda Shigetoki]], the Third Shogun who stabilized the realm after the Kinzō Incident, should be considered a "fourth unifier." However, traditional historiography maintains the triad, as Shigetoki's role was consolidating rather than creating the unified state.

==In popular culture==
{{Main|Cultural depictions of the Three Unifiers}}

The Three Unifiers have been extensively depicted in:
* ''[[The Tale of the Soga Brothers]]'' (classical epic)
* ''[[Chronicles of the Burning Temple]]'' (kabuki play, 1703)
* Numerous [[jidaigeki]] films and television dramas
* Strategy video games set in the Sengoku period

